window.NOTEIC_INDEX=[{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"New in 1.5.1 Stickers, Insert space and your toolbar colors Handwritten notes, with ink that keeps up with your pen . Noteic is a free notebook for Windows, made for the Surface Pen and other Windows pens. Real pages or a whiteboard, no account, no subscription. Get it free on the Microsoft Store Explore the guide Free, every feature Windows 10 and 11 ARM64 and x64 No account Pressure-sensitive ink Write on PDFs LaTeX formulas Works offline 45 shapes 10 paper styles 9 accent colors 0 accounts needed New in 1.5.1"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Stickers, more room and your colors Save anything you wrote as a sticker, make room on the whiteboard, recolor with your toolbar colors and switch tools with your pen. Stickers Save any selection as a sticker and place it again in any note or whiteboard, with a tap or by dragging it. How it works Insert space Make room on the whiteboard: drag down or right, and everything after the line moves along. How it works Recolor with your toolbar colors Tap Style in the selection bar and pick one of your toolbar colors. Tap it again to change that color. How it works Pen side button Hold the side button of your pen to select with the lasso or to erase, without picking another tool. How it works Also in 1.5.1 Ruler in cm or inches Search filters for subject, tag and favorites Images in new notes before saving Lock scrolling and Stop at edge Stroke previews in the pen menus Indigo and gold, and a d"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Export what you see, new shapes and your own colors Save exactly what is on screen as a PDF, draw 3D shapes and block arrows, and make every color field your own. Export what you see Save the visible area of your whiteboard as a PDF on A4 or A3, or the whole whiteboard in sheets. How it works 14 new shapes, your own colors 3D shapes, block arrows and a number line, and color fields you can change for shapes, pens and highlighters. How it works A tidier library Create, rename and color right in the list, with your own colors for subjects, chapters and tags. How it works Features"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Everything you need to think on paper From the first stroke to the finished PDF, Noteic keeps your notes fast, clean and organized. Writing Ink that keeps up with your pen Pressure, no re-sharpening when you lift the pen, and palm rejection: touch scrolls and zooms, only the pen writes. Ballpoint, fountain pen, calligraphy pen and highlighter. Pens Study Memo tape Cover answers with tape and tap to reveal them. Every note opens covered again. Memo tape Shapes Clean shapes 45 shapes, including 3D shapes, plus shape recognition, ruler and protractor. Shapes PDF Write on your PDFs Import lecture slides or worksheets, pick exactly the pages you need, write on them and export everything as a sharp vector PDF. PDF Typed text Text, tables and LaTeX Text boxes with fonts, colors, lists and checklists, tables with merged cells and LaTeX formulas with a live preview. Text, formulas and tables Orga"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Made for writing by hand Handwriting to text Select your ink with the lasso and tap Convert to text, right on your device. Lasso selection Move, scale, rotate, copy and recolor, or drag onto another page. Your own toolbar Keep the tools you use, move the rest into the drawer and put the toolbar on any edge. Ten paper styles From ruled and grid to Cornell, music and hexagon, on A6 to A3, Letter, Legal and more. Extend a page Add room next to a page or a PDF: up to 20 cm left and right, 5 cm top and bottom. Links Link handwriting to a spot in your notes, and text to the web. Tabs and windows Open notes side by side and drag a tab out or onto another window. Light and dark Follow Windows or pick a mode, with nine accent colors. Laser pointer A trail that fades by itself, for presenting from your notes. Page overview All pages at a glance, with colored bookmarks and drag to reorder. Keyboard"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Your notes are your files No account, no ads and no telemetry. Notebooks are .noteic files in a folder you choose, so OneDrive and other cloud folders just work. Read the privacy policy → Works offline Handwriting recognition runs on your device Nothing is sent to the developer Export everything as PDF at any time"},{"t":"Home","h":"","u":"","a":"","x":"Start writing today Noteic is free in the Microsoft Store. Every feature, no trial, no subscription. Get it free on the Microsoft Store Requirements"},{"t":"Download","h":"","u":"download","a":"","x":"Download Noteic is free in the Microsoft Store: every feature, no trial and no subscription. Get it free on the Microsoft Store Open in the Store app"},{"t":"Download","h":"Requirements","u":"download","a":"requirements","x":"Requirements Windows Windows 11, or Windows 10 version 2004 (build 19041) or later Processor ARM64 or x64. The Microsoft Store installs the right version for your PC. Download About 150 MB Pen Recommended: the Surface Pen or any other Windows pen. You can also write with the mouse. Account None. Noteic works offline."},{"t":"Download","h":"Install","u":"download","a":"install","x":"Install Open Noteic in the Microsoft Store . Select Get or Install . Start Noteic from the Start menu. Next: Getting started ."},{"t":"Download","h":"Updates","u":"download","a":"updates","x":"Updates The Microsoft Store keeps Noteic up to date. After a bigger update, Noteic shows once what is new. You can read all changes on What\u0027s new ."},{"t":"Download","h":"Uninstall","u":"download","a":"uninstall","x":"Uninstall Uninstalling Noteic does not delete your notes. They stay as .noteic files in your workspace, by default Documents\\Noteic . Noteic\u0027s settings, your lists of recent notes and favorites, and drafts of notes that were never saved are removed. Save your Untitled notes before you uninstall."},{"t":"Getting started","h":"","u":"guide","a":"","x":"Getting started Noteic opens with an empty page, ready for your pen. Here is how to write, find your tools and keep your notes safe."},{"t":"Getting started","h":"Write your first note","u":"guide","a":"write-your-first-note","x":"Write your first note Start Noteic from the Start menu. A window opens with a new note called Untitled . Write on the page with your pen. Touch scrolls and zooms, so your hand can rest on the screen. No pen? The mouse writes too: hold the left button and draw. To start another note, open … (More) at the end of the toolbar and choose New › Page-based document , or press Ctrl + N . For one big page that grows as you write, choose New › Whiteboard . See Whiteboard . Writing, highlighting and drawing shapes (2:48, recorded with an earlier version)"},{"t":"Getting started","h":"The toolbar","u":"guide","a":"the-toolbar","x":"The toolbar All tools sit in one toolbar. From left to right: Library Opens and closes your notes on the left. Library Pen Ballpoint, fountain pen or calligraphy pen. Tap again for its settings. Pens Highlighter Marks behind your writing. Highlighter Fill Fills closed strokes with a see-through color. Fill Eraser Erases whole strokes or an area. Eraser Shapes Lines, arrows, rectangles, stars and more. Shapes Text box Typed text, tables and formulas. Text Ruler Ruler or protractor, in centimeters or inches. Ruler and protractor Tool drawer Memo tape and laser pointer. Memo tape Lasso selection Select, move, scale, copy and recolor. On a whiteboard also Insert space. Selection Stickers Opens your stickers next to the page. Stickers Pan Drag the view with pen, finger or mouse. Paste Pastes what you copied. Widths and colors Four widths and eight colors for the current pen. Tap the active on"},{"t":"Getting started","h":"Pen, touch and mouse","u":"guide","a":"pen-touch-and-mouse","x":"Pen, touch and mouse Pen: writes and works every tool. The eraser end of your pen erases in every tool except Pan. The side button can select or erase while you hold it, see Settings . Touch: never writes. One finger scrolls, two fingers pinch to zoom, and a double tap switches between 100% and the page width. On a whiteboard, a double tap switches between 100% and everything on it. Mouse: the left button writes and works every tool. Ctrl and the wheel zoom, Shift and the wheel scroll sideways. Hold Space and drag to move the view with any tool."},{"t":"Getting started","h":"Zoom","u":"guide","a":"zoom","x":"Zoom Zoom goes from 25% to 500%; a whiteboard can zoom out further to show everything on it. When you zoom, a small zoom box appears. Tap the zoom level in it to open the zoom menu: Zoom levels from 50% to 500%. Fit : Width or Height of the page. On a whiteboard, Show all instead. Lock zoom keeps the zoom where it is. In page-based notes, Lock scrolling holds the view in all directions, Lock sideways only left and right, and Stop at edge stops scrolling at the edge of the pages."},{"t":"Getting started","h":"Where your notes are saved","u":"guide","a":"where-your-notes-are-saved","x":"Where your notes are saved Your notes are .noteic files. By default Noteic keeps them in Documents\\Noteic , your workspace . You can choose another folder, for example in OneDrive: open … › Settings and, under Library , tap Use another folder . Your notes stay where they are. Save: … › Save or Ctrl + S . A new note asks for a name and a folder the first time. Save as is Ctrl + Shift + S . Autosave: once a note has a file, Noteic saves it by itself about 20 seconds after your last change. A star (*) in the tab means there are changes not yet in the file. Untitled notes are kept as drafts and come back after a restart. After a restart, Noteic opens your windows and tabs where you left them. Tip The library is the easiest way to create and find notes: Subjects, Chapters and Notes, like a binder. See Library ."},{"t":"Pens","h":"","u":"guide/pens","a":"","x":"Pens Three pens with their own feel: ballpoint, fountain pen and calligraphy pen. Each one keeps its own widths, colors and settings."},{"t":"Pens","h":"Choose a pen","u":"guide/pens","a":"choose-a-pen","x":"Choose a pen Tap the pen button in the toolbar. It shows the pen you used last. Tap it again, or tap the small arrow at its bottom, to open the pen settings. Pick Ballpoint , Fountain pen or Calligraphy . Under each name, a short stroke shows how that pen writes. The menu closes and you can write. Ballpoint: an even line. Pressure can be turned on or off. Fountain pen: always reacts to pressure. Calligraphy: a flat nib at 45°. The line gets thick or thin with the direction of your stroke, not with pressure."},{"t":"Pens","h":"Pen settings","u":"guide/pens","a":"pen-settings","x":"Pen settings Pressure sensitivity (ballpoint and highlighter): press harder for a thicker line. On by default. Hold to straighten : at the end of a stroke, hold the pen still for about a second and the stroke becomes a straight line. It snaps to horizontal and vertical. Hold again after moving on to add a corner. Off by default. Under … › Settings › Ink prediction you choose how far the ink runs ahead of the pen tip: Off , Gentle (the default), Full or Max . More prediction shortens the gap between tip and line, but can overshoot briefly when you lift the pen. The side button of your pen can switch to the lasso or the eraser while you hold it. See Settings ."},{"t":"Pens","h":"Widths","u":"guide/pens","a":"widths","x":"Widths The toolbar shows four widths, each drawn as thick as it writes. Tap a width to use it. If another tool is active, Noteic switches back to the pen. Tap the active width again to fine-tune it with a slider in millimeters: from 0.1 mm up to 2 mm for ballpoint and fountain pen, and up to 4 mm for the calligraphy nib. Switch palette swaps between two sets of widths."},{"t":"Pens","h":"Colors","u":"guide/pens","a":"colors","x":"Colors Eight colors are in the toolbar; you can show up to ten (see Customize the toolbar ). Tap a color to use it. Tap the active color again to change it: pick one of 20 color fields. Tap a field again to make it your own with a color field, a hue bar and a Hex box for codes like #1F78EA ; Reset brings back its original color. Switch palette swaps between three palettes of ten colors each, per pen. Pen ink is always fully opaque. For see-through color, use the highlighter or Fill ."},{"t":"Highlighter","h":"","u":"guide/highlighter","a":"","x":"Highlighter The highlighter marks behind your writing, so your ink and text always stay readable on top."},{"t":"Highlighter","h":"Use the highlighter","u":"guide/highlighter","a":"use-the-highlighter","x":"Use the highlighter Tap Highlighter in the toolbar. Pick a width and a color. The highlighter has its own colors, starting with yellow, lime, pink, sky blue, orange and purple. Draw over your notes. The marks sit behind pen ink, shapes and text. Highlighter ink is see-through. Widths go up to 10 mm."},{"t":"Highlighter","h":"Settings","u":"guide/highlighter","a":"settings","x":"Settings Tap the active highlighter again to open its settings: Pressure sensitivity : on by default. Hold to straighten : hold the pen still at the end of a stroke and it becomes a straight line. Recognize shapes : draw a line, rectangle, triangle, ellipse or circle, and when you lift the pen it becomes a clean shape, still in highlighter. Tip shape : Round or Square , each with a short stroke that shows how it looks. Square, like a real marker, is the default. Tap the active highlighter color again to pick one of 20 color fields; tap a field again to change it. Tip Draw along the ruler for perfectly straight highlights. To remove only highlights, set the eraser to Highlighter . See Eraser ."},{"t":"Eraser","h":"","u":"guide/eraser","a":"","x":"Eraser Erase whole strokes or a whole area, choose what the eraser removes, or simply flip your pen."},{"t":"Eraser","h":"Erase","u":"guide/eraser","a":"erase","x":"Erase Tap Eraser in the toolbar. Its menu opens. Choose a mode: Stroke : removes every stroke you touch, as a whole. It also removes shapes when you touch their outline, and memo tape. Set the size from 0.5 to 20 mm. Area : drag a rectangle. When you lift the pen, everything it touches is removed, including text boxes and images. Erase on the page. One erase can be undone with Ctrl + Z ."},{"t":"Eraser","h":"What the eraser removes","u":"guide/eraser","a":"what-the-eraser-removes","x":"What the eraser removes Under Erases : Everything (the default) Highlighter : only highlighter, your writing stays as it is. Tape : only memo tape, your writing stays as it is. Writing under covering memo tape is protected: the eraser hits the tape, not what is underneath."},{"t":"Eraser","h":"More options","u":"guide/eraser","a":"more-options","x":"More options Deselect automatically (on by default): after erasing once, Noteic goes back to the tool you used before. Clear all erases everything on the current page. You can undo it."},{"t":"Eraser","h":"Erase with your pen","u":"guide/eraser","a":"erase-with-your-pen","x":"Erase with your pen Turn your pen around: its eraser end, or an eraser button that Windows reports as one, erases in every tool except Pan, with the mode you set. Your current tool stays selected. You can also set the side button of your pen to Eraser : hold it and erase, let go and keep writing. See Settings › Pen side button ."},{"t":"Fill and laser pointer","h":"","u":"guide/fill-and-laser","a":"","x":"Fill and laser pointer Fill gives closed strokes an inner color. The laser pointer shows where you point, without ever touching your note."},{"t":"Fill and laser pointer","h":"Fill","u":"guide/fill-and-laser","a":"fill","x":"Fill The Fill button (the bucket) is a switch for the pen and the highlighter: Tap Fill to turn it on. Its menu opens. Pick an Inner color from 20 colors and set the Opacity from 5% to 100%. Draw a closed stroke, for example a circle around a word. The inside fills with the color; the outline keeps the pen\u0027s own color. Tap Fill again to turn it off. Tap the selected color again to change it. Your fill colors are saved. Strokes drawn along the ruler are not filled. To fill shapes , use the Fill switch in the shapes menu instead."},{"t":"Fill and laser pointer","h":"Laser pointer","u":"guide/fill-and-laser","a":"laser-pointer","x":"Laser pointer The laser pointer is in the tool drawer by default. You can move it into the toolbar in Customize the toolbar . Open the tool drawer and tap Laser pointer . A check mark shows it is on. Point and draw with pen, finger or mouse. The trail stays for about two seconds and then fades. Choose another tool to turn it off. While the laser is on, the color buttons offer five laser colors: red, green, blue, yellow and pink. The trail never becomes part of your note."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"","u":"guide/selection","a":"","x":"Selection and lasso Circle what you want to change. Then move it, scale it, rotate it, copy it, give it a new color or drag it onto another page."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"Select","u":"guide/selection","a":"select","x":"Select Tap Lasso selection in the toolbar. Draw a loop around what you want. Everything completely inside the loop is selected. Tap the button again to go back to the pen. Rectangle instead of lasso: press and hold the button and choose Rectangle . On a whiteboard, the same menu has Insert space . Tap an item to select just that one. Tap empty paper to clear the selection. Add more: hold Shift while you draw or tap. Everything on the page: Ctrl + A . You can select handwriting, highlighter, shapes, text boxes, images, formulas, tables and memo tape. A selection always stays on one page."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"Move, scale and rotate","u":"guide/selection","a":"move-scale-and-rotate","x":"Move, scale and rotate Move: drag inside the frame. Release it over another page to move it there. Scale: drag a corner. Line widths scale along. Stretch: drag the middle of a side. Rotate: drag the round knob above the frame. Hold Shift for 15° steps. A single line, arrow or polygon also shows dots on its points. Drag them to reshape it; dragging the middle of a curved arrow bends it."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"The selection bar","u":"guide/selection","a":"the-selection-bar","x":"The selection bar Under the selection, a small bar offers what fits your selection: Convert to text for handwriting. See Handwriting to text . Edit text box , Edit formula or Crop image when one of those is selected. Cut , Copy and Duplicate . Save as sticker : keep the selection to place it again in any note. See Stickers . Style (the palette): line width and color, see below. Anchor link : link the selection to a spot in your notes. See Links . Delete ."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"Style and color","u":"guide/selection","a":"style-and-color","x":"Style and color Tap Style in the selection bar. A compact field opens below the selection bar, or next to it when there is no room, and never covers your selection. Line : the width in millimeters, from 0.1 to 2 mm. Colors: the same colors as in your toolbar, in the same order. Tap one to recolor the selection. Change a toolbar color: tap the marked color again, or the palette button at the end of the row. The selection and the toolbar color change together, and Noteic keeps the new color. When the selection has a color that is not in your toolbar, the palette button picks a one-time color with a color field and a Hex box. Shapes also get the line style, Fill , and Outline or Fill to choose which part you color. Highlighter stays see-through when you recolor it."},{"t":"Selection and lasso","h":"Copy and paste","u":"guide/selection","a":"copy-and-paste","x":"Copy and paste Ctrl + C , Ctrl + X and Ctrl + V work as usual. Ctrl + D duplicates. Paste lands under the pointer, or in the middle of the view. Ctrl + Shift + V , or pressing and holding the Paste button, pastes at the same position as the original. Handy for copying to another page. When you copy, Noteic also puts a picture of the selection on the Windows clipboard, so you can paste it into other apps. Delete removes the selection, Esc clears it."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"","u":"guide/shapes","a":"","x":"Shapes 45 shapes, from lines and arrows to 3D shapes and speech bubbles. Or sketch by hand and let Noteic recognize the shape."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"Draw a shape","u":"guide/shapes","a":"draw-a-shape","x":"Draw a shape Tap Shapes in the toolbar. The shapes menu opens. Pick a shape, a line width and a line style. Drag on the page from one corner to the other. Hold Shift while you drag: lines and arrows snap to 45°, a rectangle becomes a square, an ellipse a circle, and every other shape gets equal width and height."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"All shapes","u":"guide/shapes","a":"all-shapes","x":"All shapes Lines: line, arrow, double arrow, curved arrow, curved double arrow, each arrow also with solid heads, angle and axes. Brackets: curly, round and square, as one bracket or as a pair around the area you drag. Number line with a tick every 5 mm. Areas: rectangle, rounded rectangle, capsule, ellipse, triangle, rounded triangle, right triangle, diamond, parallelogram, isosceles trapezoid, pentagon, hexagon, heptagon, octagon, star and heart. Block arrows: a block arrow that points left or right as you drag, and a block double arrow. Callouts: square and rounded speech bubble, and a wavy frame. 3D shapes: cube, cylinder, sphere, cone, pyramid and prism, with the hidden edges dashed. The solid-head arrows, the number line, the heptagon, the block arrows and the 3D shapes came with Noteic 1.5."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"Drawing modes","u":"guide/shapes","a":"drawing-modes","x":"Drawing modes The top row of the menu changes how you draw: Recognize : draw freely. When you lift the pen, a line, circle, ellipse, triangle, rectangle or polygon becomes a clean shape. Draw from one shape\u0027s outline to another\u0027s and you get a clean connector. If nothing is recognized, your stroke stays as ink. Polygon : tap corner by corner; a tap on the first corner closes it. Esc discards it. Grid snapping : straight lines whose ends snap to the paper grid and to the corners of other shapes, at 0°, 45° or 90°. Spline : tap to set points, drag while setting a point to shape the curve. Tap the first point to close it, or release Esc to finish. Tap any shape in the list to go back to plain dragging."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"Line, fill and color","u":"guide/shapes","a":"line-fill-and-color","x":"Line, fill and color Line : width from 0.1 to 2 mm, or one tap on Thin lines (0.25 mm), Normal lines (0.5 mm) or Bold lines (1 mm). Seven line styles: Solid , Dashed , Long dashes , Dotted , Dash-dot , Wavy and Zigzag . Fill : fills closed shapes. Switch between Outline and Fill to give each its own color, and set the fill opacity from 5% to 100%. 20 color fields you can change: tap a field to use it, tap it again to change it. One more field at the end adds your own color. The palette button opens a custom color picker with a Hex box."},{"t":"Shapes","h":"Change a shape later","u":"guide/shapes","a":"change-a-shape-later","x":"Change a shape later Select it with the lasso . Drag the frame to move, scale or rotate it, drag its point dots to reshape it, and use Style in the selection bar to change width, line style, fill and color. See Style and color . Shape recognition and connectors (1:17)"},{"t":"Ruler and protractor","h":"","u":"guide/ruler-and-protractor","a":"","x":"Ruler and protractor Draw straight lines at any angle along the ruler, and measure and draw angles with the protractor."},{"t":"Ruler and protractor","h":"Show the ruler or protractor","u":"guide/ruler-and-protractor","a":"show-the-ruler-or-protractor","x":"Show the ruler or protractor Tap Ruler in the toolbar, or press Ctrl + L . Tap again to hide it. Press and hold the button to open its menu. Tap Ruler or Protractor and it appears right away. Only one is shown at a time."},{"t":"Ruler and protractor","h":"Ruler","u":"guide/ruler-and-protractor","a":"ruler","x":"Ruler Draw: with the pen or highlighter tool, draw along the ruler\u0027s long edge. The line follows the edge exactly, with pen pressure. Move: drag the ruler with a finger, or use the cross in the middle. Turn: drag the round arrow. The angle shows in the middle of the ruler. Length: drag the arrows at both ends. The scale shows real sizes at the current zoom, in centimeters or inches."},{"t":"Ruler and protractor","h":"Units","u":"guide/ruler-and-protractor","a":"units","x":"Units Press and hold the ruler button. Under Units , choose cm or in : cm : centimeters and millimeters. in : inches, down to 1/16 inch, with a number at every inch. Both edges of the ruler change. The change shows at once, in every open Noteic window, and is saved. Until you choose, Noteic follows your Windows region: inches where the region does not use the metric system, centimeters everywhere else. When you zoom far out, the finest marks are left out so the scale stays readable. The protractor always measures in degrees."},{"t":"Ruler and protractor","h":"Protractor","u":"guide/ruler-and-protractor","a":"protractor","x":"Protractor Draw an arc: with the pen or highlighter tool, trace along the rim. Lines from the center mark the start and the end, and the angle is shown while you draw. Move: drag the inner area with finger, pen or mouse. Size: drag the round handle with the double arrow below the center. The protractor lies on the page: it moves and zooms with the paper. Its scale runs from 0° to 359°. Formulas, ruler, protractor and splines (2:06, recorded with an earlier version)"},{"t":"Text, formulas and tables","h":"","u":"guide/text","a":"","x":"Text, formulas and tables Type next to your handwriting: text boxes with formatting, tables, and LaTeX formulas with a live preview."},{"t":"Text, formulas and tables","h":"Text boxes","u":"guide/text","a":"text-boxes","x":"Text boxes Tap Text box in the toolbar. Tap the page and type. To set the width yourself, drag sideways instead of tapping. Press Esc or tap somewhere else when you are done. An empty box disappears by itself. To edit a box later, tap it with the text tool, or double-tap it with the lasso. Move: drag the strip on top of the box. Width: drag the bars on the left and right. Size: drag the bottom right corner to scale the text. Rotate: drag the knob below the box. It snaps to 0°, 90° and 180°; hold Shift for 15° steps. Double-tap the knob to straighten the box."},{"t":"Text, formulas and tables","h":"The text toolbar","u":"guide/text","a":"the-text-toolbar","x":"The text toolbar Font , sorted into Readable, Handwriting, Code and math, and Decorative. Size from 6 to 200 pt, with Smaller and Larger . Bold , Italic and Underline ( Ctrl + B , I , U ). Text color with six favorites you can change, and Highlight with six colors. List : numbers, letters, Roman numerals, bullets, squares, dashes or a checklist. Tab and Shift + Tab indent. Alignment , Line spacing , Link and Formula . More (…): Insert table , Strikethrough , Clear formatting , Set as default and Delete text box . New text boxes start in Segoe UI, 12 pt, black. Set as default makes your current font, size and color the new default. Paste with formatting Copy text from a website, Word or OneNote and paste it onto the page (with no text box open): Noteic creates a new text box and keeps bold, italic, headings, lists and links."},{"t":"Text, formulas and tables","h":"Formulas","u":"guide/text","a":"formulas","x":"Formulas In a text box, tap Formula in the text toolbar. Type LaTeX, for example x_{1,2} = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} . The preview shows the result as you type. Or tap the building blocks under Structures , Greek , Relations and Sets . Tab jumps to the next empty box. Tap the check mark or press Ctrl + Enter . The formula is placed as its own object just below the text box, in the box\u0027s text color. To change it, select it with the lasso and tap Edit formula , or double-tap it."},{"t":"Text, formulas and tables","h":"Tables","u":"guide/text","a":"tables","x":"Tables In a text box, open More (…) › Insert table . Choose a size, up to 6 × 6 to start. The table appears below the box. Type in the cells. Tab goes to the next cell; Tab in the last cell adds a row. In a cell, More (…) inserts and deletes rows and columns, merges cells to the right or below, splits merged cells and sets a Cell color . Tables have up to 10 columns."},{"t":"Memo tape","h":"","u":"guide/memo-tape","a":"","x":"Memo tape Cover terms, answers or labels with tape, then tap the tape to see what is underneath. Every note opens covered again, ready for the next round."},{"t":"Memo tape","h":"Cover with tape","u":"guide/memo-tape","a":"cover-with-tape","x":"Cover with tape Open the tool drawer in the toolbar and tap Memo tape . Drag over the writing you want to cover. While memo tape is active, the width and color buttons in the toolbar set the tape: widths from 5 to 14 mm and ten tape colors."},{"t":"Memo tape","h":"Reveal","u":"guide/memo-tape","a":"reveal","x":"Reveal Tap a tape with your finger, with any tool, to reveal or cover it again. With the memo tape tool active, a short tap with the pen or mouse works too. Writing over a covered tape reveals it. Show all answers reveals every tape of the note at once. What you revealed is not saved: the note always opens covered."},{"t":"Memo tape","h":"Options","u":"guide/memo-tape","a":"options","x":"Options Straight tape (on by default): the tape runs straight from start to end; Shift snaps it to 45°. Turn it off to make the tape follow your stroke. Shape : Round or Washi ends. Eight patterns: Plain, Dots, Stripes, Checks, Zigzag, Diagonal, Gingham and Stars. To remove only tape, set the eraser to Tape . In a PDF export, every tape is covered. You can put memo tape into the toolbar in Customize the toolbar . Memo tape (0:24, recorded with an earlier version, when it was called Study tape) Learning with memo tape (1:05, recorded with an earlier version)"},{"t":"Stickers","h":"","u":"guide/stickers","a":"","x":"Stickers New in 1.5.1 Save anything you wrote or drew as a sticker, for example a star, a check mark or an “Exam!” label, and place it again in any note or whiteboard."},{"t":"Stickers","h":"Open your stickers","u":"guide/stickers","a":"open-your-stickers","x":"Open your stickers Tap Stickers in the toolbar, right after the lasso. The Stickers panel opens on the right, next to the page, and shows how many stickers you have. The newest sticker is on top. Tap the × or the Stickers button again to close it. In a narrow window, the panel floats over the right side of the page instead."},{"t":"Stickers","h":"Place a sticker","u":"guide/stickers","a":"place-a-sticker","x":"Place a sticker Tap a sticker: it lands in the middle of the view, already selected, so you can move it right away. Drag a sticker onto the page with the pen or the mouse. With a finger, press and hold the sticker first, then drag. A preview in real size shows where it lands: “Release to place”. Stickers work on pages and on whiteboards. A sticker becomes normal content of your note: move, scale, rotate or recolor it with the lasso . One Ctrl + Z takes it away again. Esc cancels a drag."},{"t":"Stickers","h":"Make your own","u":"guide/stickers","a":"make-your-own","x":"Make your own Select handwriting, shapes, text, images or anything else with the lasso . Tap Save as sticker in the selection bar (the sticker symbol with a +). Or drag the selection into the open Stickers panel: “Release to save sticker”. Your selection stays where it is on the page. The panel then shows “Sticker saved” with Undo . The new sticker gets a name from what is in it, such as Handwriting, Highlight, Shapes, Image or Text."},{"t":"Stickers","h":"Organize stickers","u":"guide/stickers","a":"organize-stickers","x":"Organize stickers Menu: right-click a sticker, or press and hold it, for Rename , Move to top and Remove from stickers . Reorder: drag a sticker inside the panel to a new place (“Move here”). The order is kept. Remove: drag a sticker onto Remove from stickers at the bottom of the panel, or use the menu. It goes to the Windows Recycle Bin, and Undo brings it back. Stickers you already placed in your notes stay."},{"t":"Stickers","h":"Where stickers are saved","u":"guide/stickers","a":"where-stickers-are-saved","x":"Where stickers are saved Stickers live in your workspace, in a hidden folder next to your notes. Each sticker is a small .noteic file, so stickers sync with OneDrive just like your notes. Links are not part of a sticker. Don\u0027t need stickers? Move the button into the tool drawer or turn it off in Customize the toolbar . When it is off, Save as sticker is hidden too."},{"t":"Links","h":"","u":"guide/links","a":"","x":"Links Jump from a word to the page where you explain it, or from typed text to a website."},{"t":"Links","h":"Link handwriting to a spot","u":"guide/links","a":"link-handwriting-to-a-spot","x":"Link handwriting to a spot Select the handwriting or shape with the lasso . Tap Anchor link in the selection bar, or press Ctrl + K . Go to the target, on any page of the note, and press and hold there. Linked ink gets a dashed blue underline with a small chain. Tap Anchor link on it again for Go to anchor , Move anchor and Remove link ."},{"t":"Links","h":"Link text","u":"guide/links","a":"link-text","x":"Link text In a text box, select a word and tap Link in the text toolbar, or press Ctrl + K . Type a web address or an e-mail address and tap Insert link . Or tap Link to a spot and tap the target in your notes. Text links are blue and underlined. To change one, put the cursor in it and press Ctrl + K ."},{"t":"Links","h":"Follow a link","u":"guide/links","a":"follow-a-link","x":"Follow a link Tap linked ink with your finger, or point at it with the lasso: a small bar shows the target and Open link . Web and e-mail links open in your browser or e-mail program. Links to a spot scroll right to it."},{"t":"Handwriting to text","h":"","u":"guide/handwriting-to-text","a":"","x":"Handwriting to text Select your handwriting and tap once: Noteic turns it into a text box you can edit, right where you wrote."},{"t":"Handwriting to text","h":"Before you start","u":"guide/handwriting-to-text","a":"before-you-start","x":"Before you start Noteic uses the handwriting recognition built into Windows. It runs on your device and works offline. Add handwriting for your language once: Open Windows Settings › Time \u0026 language › Language \u0026 region . Next to your language, select … › Language options . Under Handwriting , install handwriting if Windows offers a download. If no recognition is installed, Noteic says so: “No handwriting recognition — see Windows Settings › Time \u0026 language”."},{"t":"Handwriting to text","h":"Convert","u":"guide/handwriting-to-text","a":"convert","x":"Convert Select your handwriting with the lasso . Tap Convert to text in the selection bar. Your ink is replaced by one text box in the same place, with one line per line you wrote. The text gets your default text box font, a size that matches your writing, and your ink color. Words in another color keep their color. Slanted writing is recognized too. Changed your mind? Ctrl + Z brings your handwriting back."},{"t":"Handwriting to text","h":"Correct a word","u":"guide/handwriting-to-text","a":"correct-a-word","x":"Correct a word Right after converting, while the new text box is still selected, tap a word. A small bar shows your original ink for that word, up to four suggestions and Edit text . Shape recognition and Convert to text (1:17)"},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"","x":"Pages and paper Ten paper styles on real page sizes, with your own colors. Extend a page when you need more room, and keep track of every page in the overview."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Open the page menu","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"open-the-page-menu","x":"Open the page menu Open … › Settings and, under Current page , tap Paper \u0026 size . Every change applies to the current page and can be undone. New pages copy the size and paper of the page before them."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Size","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"size","x":"Size Paper size : A6, A5, A4, A3, Letter, Legal, Tabloid, B5 (JIS), F4 and Oficio. Portrait or Landscape ."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Paper","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"paper","x":"Paper Each paper style shows a live preview in your colors: Blank , Ruled , Grid and Dots Cornell : a cue column on the left and a summary at the bottom Isometric Graph : lines every 1, 5 and 10 mm Music : staves Writing : top line, dashed middle and baseline Hexagon Below the styles: Spacing from 3 to 20 mm (the space between staves for music, the line height for writing paper). Line weight : Thin lines , Normal lines or Bold lines . Margin line : none, left, right or both, with its own color and distance from the edge. Background color and Line color : five favorites each, plus a custom color with a Hex box. Tap a favorite to use it, tap it again to change it."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Paper for new notes","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"paper-for-new-notes","x":"Paper for new notes At the bottom, New notes start with shows the page new notes get, for example “Grid · A4 · White”. Tap Use this page to make the current page the template. Existing notes do not change."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Extend a page","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"extend-a-page","x":"Extend a page Need more room next to a page or a PDF? Under Extend page borders : Choose This page or All pages . Add room in steps of 1 cm: up to 5 cm at the top and bottom, up to 20 cm on the left and right. The new area uses the page\u0027s paper. Reset shrinks the borders back to your content. Pages with wider borders stay centered under each other."},{"t":"Pages and paper","h":"Page overview","u":"guide/pages-and-paper","a":"page-overview","x":"Page overview Tap the page number in the toolbar, or press Ctrl + G , to see all pages at once. The current page has a colored ring. Tap a page to jump to it. Reorder: drag a page by its handle, or press and hold it. Bookmarks: the ⋮ menu under each page sets one of 15 bookmark colors. Bookmarked at the top shows only pages with bookmarks, All shows every page again. Page commands in the same menu: Insert page after , Duplicate page , Move page to the beginning or to the end , Copy page to… and Move page to… another note, and Delete page . Type a page number in the field at the top to go there. Between pages, a small bar moves the page above up or down, inserts a page or deletes one. Page Up and Page Down scroll page by page."},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"","x":"Whiteboard New in 1.5 One page that grows on all four sides as you write. Brainstorms, mind maps and sketches get all the room they need, with the same pens, shapes, text boxes, images and stickers as your notes."},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"Create a whiteboard","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"create-a-whiteboard","x":"Create a whiteboard In the library: tap + next to Note and choose Whiteboard . Type a name right in the list and press Enter . From the menu: open … › New › Whiteboard . A new whiteboard opens in its own tab. In the library, a whiteboard says Whiteboard under its name. It is a .noteic file like every other note."},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"Room in every direction","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"room-in-every-direction","x":"Room in every direction A whiteboard starts as one landscape A4 sheet. Write, draw or move things near an edge and the page grows on that side. It can grow up to about 5 m to the left and to the right, 1 m up and 20 m down, so about 10.3 × 21.2 m in all. When a side reaches its maximum size, Noteic tells you. Everything else works as on a page: pens, highlighter, eraser, shapes, text boxes, formulas, tables, images, memo tape, links and stickers . A whiteboard always has exactly one page, so the page overview and Import PDF are only in page-based notes."},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"See everything","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"see-everything","x":"See everything Tap Whiteboard in the toolbar, where page-based notes show the page number. Or open the zoom menu and tap Show all . Or double-tap the whiteboard with your finger. Double-tap again to go back to 100%. Show all fits everything you wrote into the window, up to 100%. A whiteboard can zoom out further than 25% for this."},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"Insert space","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"insert-space","x":"Insert space Need room between two ideas? Insert space pushes everything after a line out of the way, like in a paper notebook you could stretch. Press and hold Lasso selection in the toolbar. Choose Insert space (“Drag down or right”). It is only in the menu of a whiteboard. Put the pen or mouse down where you want the room and drag. Drag down: everything below the line moves down, across the whole width of the whiteboard. Drag right: everything right of the line moves right. Your first movement decides the direction. A band in your accent color shows the room, with its size in millimeters. The content moves when you lift the pen. Drag up or left to take room away. It stops at the nearest content, so nothing ends up on top of each other. What the line crosses stays where it is. One move is one step: Ctrl + Z undoes it. Insert space stays on until you choose another tool. To select again"},{"t":"Whiteboard","h":"Export as PDF","u":"guide/whiteboard","a":"export-as-pdf","x":"Export as PDF Open … › Export as PDF . Under Save as type , choose what to save. Choose a name and a folder and save. PDF, visible area on A4 or A3 What you see on screen, fitted onto one sheet. This is the default. PDF, whole whiteboard in A4 or A3 sheets The whole whiteboard at full size, cut into portrait or landscape sheets. Noteic remembers your choice for the next export. The PDF is a vector PDF, sharp at any zoom. Zoom out first to put more of your whiteboard onto one sheet. Older versions of Noteic If you open a whiteboard with Noteic 1.3 or 1.4 on another PC, it shows a note to update Noteic in the Microsoft Store to see and edit all of it."},{"t":"PDF","h":"","u":"guide/pdf","a":"","x":"PDF Bring lecture slides and worksheets into your notes, write right on them and export the result as a PDF."},{"t":"PDF","h":"Import a PDF","u":"guide/pdf","a":"import-a-pdf","x":"Import a PDF Save your note first, because the PDF is stored inside the note. Open … › Import PDF and choose the file. Pick the pages: tap pages to select them, use All and None , or type a range like 1-3, 7, 10-12 . All pages are selected at first. Tap Import . The pages are added after the current page, each in its own PDF size. If the note only has one empty page, that page is replaced. You can undo the import. New note from a PDF: in the library , right-click or press and hold a Chapter and choose Import PDF… . Noteic creates a new note named after the PDF."},{"t":"PDF","h":"Write on PDF pages","u":"guide/pdf","a":"write-on-pdf-pages","x":"Write on PDF pages The PDF is the page background: every tool writes on top of it. Use Extend page borders to add room around a PDF page; the paper style you pick fills the new area."},{"t":"PDF","h":"Export as PDF","u":"guide/pdf","a":"export-as-pdf","x":"Export as PDF Open … › Export as PDF . Pick the pages, or tap Only this page . Choose where to save. When it is done, Noteic offers to open the file. The export is a vector PDF: sharp at any zoom. It contains your paper and patterns, imported PDF pages, images, ink, highlighter, fills, shapes, text, formulas and memo tape (covered). On a whiteboard , you choose under Save as type instead: the visible area on A4 or A3, or the whole whiteboard in sheets. See Whiteboard › Export as PDF . Import PDF is only in page-based notes."},{"t":"PDF","h":"Images","u":"guide/pdf","a":"images","x":"Images … › Import image adds a PNG, JPG, GIF, BMP, TIFF or WebP file. Drag an image from File Explorer or from a website into your note. Paste an image from the clipboard with Ctrl + V . Images are stored inside the note. This also works in a new note you have not saved yet; only a PDF needs a saved note. Move, scale and rotate images with the lasso ; Crop image in the selection bar crops without losing the original."},{"t":"Library","h":"","u":"guide/library","a":"","x":"Library Your notes, sorted like a binder: Subjects, Chapters and Notes, with colors, icons, tags and favorites. Create, rename and color everything right in the list."},{"t":"Library","h":"Open the library","u":"guide/library","a":"open-the-library","x":"Open the library Tap the library button at the start of the toolbar, or press Ctrl + B . Drag its edge to make it wider: a narrow library shows one or two columns, a wide one shows Subject, Chapter and Note side by side. The icons on the left switch between Library , Search , Recently opened , Favorites and Notes by Tags ."},{"t":"Library","h":"Subjects, Chapters and Notes","u":"guide/library","a":"subjects-chapters-and-notes","x":"Subjects, Chapters and Notes A Subject (for example a course) holds Chapters (for example weeks), and Chapters hold your Notes. On disk they are simply folders and .noteic files in your workspace. Tap + next to Subject to create a Subject. Tap + next to Chapter to add a Chapter to it. Tap + next to Note and choose Page-based document or Whiteboard . A name field appears right in the list, at the place where the new item will be. Type the name and press Enter , or tap next to it. Esc cancels, and nothing is created. A tap on a note selects it. To open it, right-click or press and hold the note and choose Open in new tab , Open in background tab or Open in new window ."},{"t":"Library","h":"The note menu","u":"guide/library","a":"the-note-menu","x":"The note menu Right-click or press and hold a note for: Add to Favorites , Rename , Move to… and Assign Tags Make sub-note of … : tucks a note under the one above it. Make main Note undoes it. Show in Explorer and Move to Recycle Bin Rename turns the row into a name field. You can also press F2 to rename the item you tapped last. Subjects and Chapters have Color and Subjects an Icon , 140 icons sorted by field of study. Color opens 16 color fields right under the row: tap one to use it, tap it again to change it, or choose No color . Subjects, Chapters and tags each keep their own 16 colors. Chapters also offer Import PDF… . Drag the handle next to a row to put notes, Chapters and Subjects in your own order. Deleted items go to the Windows Recycle Bin, so you can restore them from there."},{"t":"Library","h":"Search and filters","u":"guide/library","a":"search-and-filters","x":"Search and filters Tap Search on the left and type. Search finds Notes and Chapters in the whole workspace by name and marks the match in yellow. Below the search field, three filters narrow the list, with or without text: All or Favorites . All subjects or one Subject. All tags or one tag."},{"t":"Library","h":"Recent, favorites and tags","u":"guide/library","a":"recent-favorites-and-tags","x":"Recent, favorites and tags Recently opened lists your last 20 notes. Favorites lists the notes you starred. Tags: in the tags view, tap + to add a tag. It gets the next free of 16 colors. Give tags to notes with Assign Tags , and tap a tag to see its notes. Right-click or press and hold a tag for Rename , Color and Delete ."},{"t":"Library","h":"Workspaces","u":"guide/library","a":"workspaces","x":"Workspaces A workspace is a folder with all your notes, for example one per semester. Each one keeps its own recent notes and favorites. Open the Subject switcher at the top of the library (or the workspace button above the Subjects in the wide view) and tap the workspace row. Pick a workspace from the list, or tap Add Workspace… to choose another folder. Nothing is moved. The × on a workspace removes it from the list. The folder and its notes stay untouched. Opening and closing the library (0:14)"},{"t":"Tabs and windows","h":"","u":"guide/tabs-and-windows","a":"","x":"Tabs and windows Keep several notes open in tabs, or put two notes side by side in their own windows."},{"t":"Tabs and windows","h":"Tabs","u":"guide/tabs-and-windows","a":"tabs","x":"Tabs Every note you open gets a tab. Close it with its ×, a middle click, or Ctrl + W . Drag tabs to reorder them. Ctrl + Tab goes to the tab you used last, Ctrl + Page Down and Ctrl + Page Up to the next and previous tab. With many tabs, the tab bar scrolls and an arrow lists all tabs. Noteic keeps up to 12 notes open. When you open more, it saves and closes the tab you used longest ago. Untitled notes are never closed this way."},{"t":"Tabs and windows","h":"Windows","u":"guide/tabs-and-windows","a":"windows","x":"Windows … › New window moves the current tab into its own window. Or drag a tab down out of the tab bar: it opens in a new window where you drop it. To move a tab into another Noteic window, drag it onto the tab bar of that window. A mark shows where it lands. Or use … › Move to other window . When you close a window while another is open, its tabs move to the other window. The window title shows where the note lives, for example “Noteic › Statistics › Week 2 › Lecture”."},{"t":"Tabs and windows","h":"Full screen","u":"guide/tabs-and-windows","a":"full-screen","x":"Full screen … › Full screen or F11 hides the title bar and scroll bars; tabs and toolbar stay. Press F11 again to leave it."},{"t":"Customize the toolbar","h":"","u":"guide/toolbar","a":"","x":"Customize the toolbar Keep only the tools you use. Move the rest into the drawer or hide them, and put the toolbar where it suits you."},{"t":"Customize the toolbar","h":"Choose your tools","u":"guide/toolbar","a":"choose-your-tools","x":"Choose your tools Open … › Settings › Customize toolbar . For each tool, choose Toolbar , Drawer or Off . Set how many Widths (up to 4) and Colors (up to 10) the toolbar shows, or turn them off. You can set Library, Highlighter, Fill, Eraser, Shapes, Text box, Ruler, Lasso selection, Pan, Paste, Memo tape, Laser pointer and Stickers. Library and Pan can go into the drawer but not off. The pen, Undo and Redo always stay. Memo tape and the laser pointer start in the drawer. Stickers start in the toolbar, right after the lasso. Reset brings back the standard toolbar."},{"t":"Customize the toolbar","h":"The tool drawer","u":"guide/toolbar","a":"the-tool-drawer","x":"The tool drawer Tools set to Drawer wait behind the drawer button in the toolbar. Tap it to open the drawer and pick a tool. The drawer button only shows when something is in it."},{"t":"Customize the toolbar","h":"Move the toolbar","u":"guide/toolbar","a":"move-the-toolbar","x":"Move the toolbar Drag the grip at the start of the toolbar to the top, bottom, left or right edge of the window. Noteic keeps the toolbar where you put it. The toolbar never runs past the edge of the window. When the window is too small for every tool, the toolbar scrolls."},{"t":"Settings","h":"","u":"guide/settings","a":"","x":"Settings All settings are under … › Settings , in the More menu at the end of the toolbar. The arrow at the top takes you back to the menu."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Appearance","u":"guide/settings","a":"appearance","x":"Appearance Light , Dark or System . System follows your Windows setting and is the default. Accent color : Blue, Indigo, Purple, Pink, Orange, Gold, Green, Petrol or Graphite. It colors Noteic itself, never your ink or paper. Customize toolbar chooses which tools you see. See Customize the toolbar . To move the toolbar to another edge of the window, drag its grip. See Move the toolbar ."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Pen and ink","u":"guide/settings","a":"pen-and-ink","x":"Pen and ink Ink prediction : Off , Gentle , Full or Max (8, 16 or 24 ms ahead). The ink runs a little ahead of the pen tip, which shortens the visible gap, but can overshoot briefly when you lift the pen. Gentle is the default. Pen side button : what the side button of your pen does while you hold it. Off (the default): nothing special in Noteic. Lasso : hold the button and circle what you want to select. The selection bar appears. If nothing was caught, your previous tool is back. Eraser : hold the button to erase, let go to write again. Top button : the top button of your pen is set up in Windows. Set it up in Windows opens Windows Settings › Bluetooth \u0026 devices › Pen \u0026 Windows Ink."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Text","u":"guide/settings","a":"text","x":"Text Default text box font shows the font, size and color for new text boxes. To change it, set up a text box the way you like and choose … › Set as default in its toolbar. Reset goes back to Segoe UI, 12 pt, black."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Library","u":"guide/settings","a":"library","x":"Library Shows the folder where your notes live, by default Documents\\Noteic . Open in Explorer opens the folder. Use another folder picks another folder as the workspace. Your notes stay where they are. To keep several workspaces, for example one per semester, see Library › Workspaces ."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Current page","u":"guide/settings","a":"current-page","x":"Current page Paper \u0026 size opens the page menu for background, grid and page size. See Pages and paper ."},{"t":"Settings","h":"Advanced","u":"guide/settings","a":"advanced","x":"Advanced Diagnostics turns on a display of performance and memory usage."},{"t":"Settings","h":"About Noteic","u":"guide/settings","a":"about-noteic","x":"About Noteic … › About Noteic shows your version, for example 1.5.1.0, and a link to r/Noteic ."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"","x":"Keyboard shortcuts Noteic is made for the pen, but these keys save time when a keyboard is attached."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Files","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"files","x":"Files Ctrl + N New page-based document Ctrl + O Open Ctrl + S Save Ctrl + Shift + S Save as"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Editing","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"editing","x":"Editing Ctrl + Z Undo Ctrl + Y Redo Ctrl + X , Ctrl + C , Ctrl + V Cut, copy, paste Ctrl + Shift + V Paste at the same position as the original Ctrl + D Duplicate the selection Ctrl + A Select everything on the page Delete Delete the selection Esc Clear the selection, leave the Pan tool, cancel a polygon Ctrl + K Anchor link for the selection, or a link in text Shift While drawing shapes: 45°, squares and circles. While rotating: 15° steps"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"View and pages","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"view-and-pages","x":"View and pages Ctrl + + , Ctrl + - Zoom in, zoom out Ctrl + 0 Zoom to 100% Space + drag Move the view with any tool Page Up , Page Down Previous and next page Ctrl + Home , Ctrl + End First and last page Ctrl + G Page overview Ctrl + L Show or hide the ruler or protractor Ctrl + B Library (in a text box: bold) F11 Full screen"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Library","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"library","x":"Library F2 Rename the Note, Chapter, Subject or tag you tapped last Enter Confirm a new name Esc Cancel a new name"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Tabs","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"tabs","x":"Tabs Ctrl + Tab Tab you used last Ctrl + Page Down , Ctrl + Page Up Next and previous tab Ctrl + W Close the tab"},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","h":"Text boxes","u":"guide/shortcuts","a":"text-boxes","x":"Text boxes Ctrl + B , Ctrl + I , Ctrl + U Bold, italic, underline Tab , Shift + Tab Indent lists; in tables, next and previous cell Esc Close the text box Ctrl + Enter Insert the formula (in the formula editor)"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"","u":"whats-new","a":"","x":"What\u0027s new Noteic updates through the Microsoft Store. Here is what each version brought."},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.5.1 Latest","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-5-1","x":"Version 1.5.1 Latest Stickers Insert space on the whiteboard Recolor with your toolbar colors Pen side button Stickers : tap Stickers next to the lasso, then tap a sticker or drag it onto your page or whiteboard. Make your own stickers: select with the lasso and tap Save as sticker , or drag the selection into the Stickers panel. Insert space on the whiteboard: press and hold the lasso button, choose Insert space , then drag down or right to make room. Recolor with your toolbar colors : tap Style in the selection bar and pick a color. Tap it again to change that toolbar color. Pen side button : hold it to select with the lasso or to erase. Choose under … › Settings › Pen side button . Ruler in cm or inches : press and hold the ruler button and choose under Units . Search filters for subject, tag and favorites in the library. Add images to a new note before you save it. Lock scrolling and"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.5 24 October 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-5","x":"Version 1.5 24 October 2026 Whiteboard Export what you see New shapes and your own colors What else? Whiteboard : one page that grows on all four sides as you write, up to about 10.3 × 21.2 m. Tap + in the library and choose Whiteboard , or open … › New › Whiteboard . Show all , or double-tap with your finger, to see everything on your whiteboard. Export what you see : the visible area as PDF on A4 or A3, or the whole whiteboard in sheets. Choose under Save as type . 14 new shapes in a new shapes menu, including 3D shapes, block arrows and a number line. Color fields you can change for shapes, pens and highlighters. A tidier library : create and rename ( F2 ) right in the list, and color subjects, chapters and tags with 16 colors you can change. Swiping stays straight, and a clearer zoom menu with Lock sideways . Extend page borders up to 20 cm left and right, and pages stay centered und"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.4 14 October 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-4","x":"Version 1.4 14 October 2026 Handwriting to text : select your ink with the lasso and tap Convert to text . Noteic uses the Windows handwriting recognition for your language, on your device. Customize your toolbar : keep the tools you use, move the rest into the drawer or hide them. New paper styles : graph, music, writing and hexagon paper, with line weight, your own colors and a page menu with live previews. A calmer gray light mode, clearer tabs and easier-to-read dialogs. Petrol as a new accent color, and menus always stay inside the Noteic window."},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.3 8 October 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-3","x":"Version 1.3 8 October 2026 Extend a page on all four sides for more room, also around PDF pages with a paper pattern Tables for typed text: rows, columns, merged cells and cell colors A new text toolbar with links, line spacing, more fonts and a sixth highlight color Text boxes: move them by the strip on top and rotate them with the knob below Text pasted from websites, Word and OneNote keeps its formatting Memo tape (formerly Study tape): washi ends and three new patterns Tidier menus in blocks that scroll with pen, finger and mouse and never cover the taskbar Page overview: 15 brighter bookmark colors and a clearer page menu Choose the toolbar position on a simple layout Rounder shapes and all memo tape patterns in the PDF export A PDF page keeps its PDF when you pick a paper pattern"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.2 5 October 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-2","x":"Version 1.2 5 October 2026 Study tape: cover answers and tap to reveal them Links on your handwriting to a spot in your notes Drag a selection onto another page Smoother writing with mouse and tablet Smoother scrolling and a smoother library The folder path of the note in the window title Closing a window moves its tabs to another window A warmer light mode and a pink laser pointer Less memory and CPU use “About Noteic” in the “…” menu"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.1 2 October 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-1","x":"Version 1.1 2 October 2026 Noteic is now free: every feature, no trial, no subscription Workspaces: switch between note folders, for example one per semester Sub-notes, like subpages Library: your own order with drag handles for pen, finger and mouse; a tap on a note selects it, its menu opens it; tags on their own line; 24 folder colors plus custom colors; 70 subject icons Fill: one tap on the fill button turns it on or off; the 20 fill colors can be changed and are saved On x64 devices with external pen tablets, strokes no longer look thicker while you write Slightly larger toolbar"},{"t":"What\u0027s new","h":"Version 1.0 29 September 2026","u":"whats-new","a":"v1-0","x":"Version 1.0 29 September 2026 First release in the Microsoft Store."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"","u":"privacy","a":"","x":"Noteic Privacy Policy Effective date: 28 September 2026 Noteic is a page-based note-taking app for Windows, developed by Stefan Bogdanovic. This policy explains which data Noteic processes and where it goes."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"In short","u":"privacy","a":"in-short","x":"In short Noteic itself sends no data to the developer. It has no user accounts, no advertising, no analytics and no telemetry. It goes online only when you ask it to, as described below."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Your notes","u":"privacy","a":"your-notes","x":"Your notes Your notebooks are saved as .noteic files in folders you choose on your device. If you choose a folder that a cloud service keeps in sync (for example OneDrive), that service transfers your files under its own privacy policy. Noteic itself does not upload anything."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Data kept on your device","u":"privacy","a":"data-kept-on-your-device","x":"Data kept on your device Noteic stores its settings, the list of recently used notebooks, recovery copies of unsaved work and technical logs (error and performance reports) in its app data folders on your device. These files stay on your device unless you pass them on yourself."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Internet access","u":"privacy","a":"internet-access","x":"Internet access Noteic connects to the internet only when you ask it to: Images from the web. When you drag an image from a website into a note, or paste a link to an image, Noteic downloads that image directly from the website. The website receives an ordinary web request, like from a browser: it includes your IP address and identifies the program as “Noteic”. Links in notes. When you click a web or e-mail link in a note, Noteic hands it to your default browser or e-mail program. Noteic sends no other data over the network."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Microsoft Store","u":"privacy","a":"microsoft-store","x":"Microsoft Store Microsoft gives developers statistics and error reports about their apps, such as the number of installs and reports of crashes, according to your Windows diagnostic data settings. This is covered by the Microsoft Privacy Statement . The developer uses these reports only to fix errors."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Children","u":"privacy","a":"children","x":"Children Noteic does not collect data from anyone, including children."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Changes to this policy","u":"privacy","a":"changes-to-this-policy","x":"Changes to this policy If this policy changes, the new version will be published at this address with a new effective date."},{"t":"Privacy","h":"Contact","u":"privacy","a":"contact","x":"Contact Questions about this policy: Stefan Bogdanovic, bogdaste@gmail.com"},{"t":"Support","h":"","u":"support","a":"","x":"Support Questions, ideas or something that does not work? Get in touch."},{"t":"Support","h":"E-mail","u":"support","a":"e-mail","x":"E-mail Write to bogdaste@gmail.com . If something goes wrong, tell us your Noteic version ( … › About Noteic ), whether your PC is ARM64 or x64, and what you did just before."},{"t":"Support","h":"Community","u":"support","a":"community","x":"Community Share ideas and ask other users on r/Noteic on Reddit."},{"t":"Support","h":"Rate Noteic","u":"support","a":"rate-noteic","x":"Rate Noteic If you like Noteic, a rating in the Microsoft Store helps others find it."}];
